// {{sourceName}}: a starter Malloy model over {{dataPath}}, served by Publisher.
//
// CHECK THE ROW COUNT BEFORE YOU TRUST THIS. A spreadsheet is only a table when
// its first row is the header and the rows under it are data. Plenty are not:
// a report title, a "generated on" banner, a merged group header, or a blank
// line above the header are all normal in an export, and they are all invisible
// here. DuckDB takes the first row it finds as the column names and stops at the
// first blank row, so a workbook with thousands of rows can load as a handful,
// or as one, and nothing reports an error. The server says it is serving, the
// query returns 200, and the number is wrong.
//
// So run `overview` first and compare `record_count` against what you know is in
// the file. If it is short, the read is wrong, and this is the fix: name the
// sheet and say where the real header starts. Replace the source below with
//
//   source: {{sourceName}} is duckdb.sql("""
//     SELECT * FROM read_xlsx(
//       '{{dataPath}}',
//       sheet = 'Sheet1',      -- only the FIRST sheet is read unless you name one
//       header = true,
//       range = 'A5:Z100000'   -- start at the row your real header is on
//     )
//   """)
//
// `duckdb.table()` takes a plain path and nothing else, which is why anything
// needing an option goes through the SQL form above. Other things a real
// spreadsheet does: a column mixing numbers and text fails at query time rather
// than at load (add `ignore_errors = true`, or `all_varchar = true` and cast
// where you define measures), currency and percent cells arrive as text, and
// every number is a double. `skill:malloy-gotchas-modeling` has the full list.
//
// Beyond that this is the same starting point as any seeded package: the
// scaffolder does not read your columns, so it begins with a row count. Build it
// out by adding dimensions and measures for the columns you care about. Validate
// edits with malloy_compile and run new views by name with malloy_reloadPackage.

source: {{sourceName}} is duckdb.table('{{dataPath}}') extend {
  measure: record_count is count()

  view: overview is {
    aggregate: record_count
  }
}
